\begin_layout Title
Microeconomic Theory I: Homework 11
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*

\bar under
Section 4.4, Problem 1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is the production funciton associated with a single-output technology,
 and let 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 be the associated production set.
 Prove that 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 satisfies Constant Returns of Scale if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is homogenous of degree one.
 Conclude that in the single-input single-output case CRS implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is linear.
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Given:
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $f:\mathbb{R}^{L-1}\rightarrow\mathbb{\mathbb{R}}_{+}$
\end_inset

 is the production functionc associated with a single-output technology.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $Y\equiv\left\{ (-z,q)\in\mathbb{R}^{L}:z\in\mathbb{R}^{L-1}\ \mbox{and}\ q-f(z)=0\right\} $
\end_inset


\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Show:
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ y\in Y,\ \forall\ \alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{+}:\alpha y\in Y\iff\forall\ \alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{+},\ \forall\ z\in\mathbb{R}^{L-1}:\alpha f(z)=f(\alpha z)$
\end_inset


\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Proof:
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $(\Rightarrow)$
\end_inset

 Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ y\in Y,\ \forall\ \alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{+}:\alpha y\in Y$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{+},\ z\in\mathbb{R}^{L-1}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\emph on
Want to show
\emph default
:
\series default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha f(z)=f(\alpha z)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For this 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

, let 
\begin_inset Formula $f(z)=q\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So, 
\begin_inset Formula $(-z,q)\in\partial f\equiv\left\{ (-z,q)\in\mathbb{R}^{L}:z\in\mathbb{R}^{L-1}\ \mbox{and}\ q-f(z)=0\right\} \subseteq Y$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 satisfies CRS, 
\begin_inset Formula $(-\alpha z,\alpha q)\in Y\implies\alpha q\leq f(\alpha z)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $f(\alpha z)=\tilde{q}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}\implies(-\alpha z,\tilde{q})\in Y$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since Y satisfies CRS, 
\begin_inset Formula $\left(-z,\frac{\tilde{q}}{\alpha}\right)\in Y\implies\frac{\tilde{q}}{\alpha}\leq f(z)=q$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Note, 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha q\leq f(\alpha z)=\tilde{q}\implies q\leq\frac{\tilde{q}}{\alpha}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, 
\begin_inset Formula $q\leq\frac{\tilde{q}}{\alpha}\ \wedge\ q\geq\frac{\tilde{q}}{\alpha}\implies q=\frac{\tilde{q}}{\alpha}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $f(z)=f(\alpha z)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{+},\ \forall\ z\in\mathbb{R}^{L-1}:\alpha f(z)=f(\alpha z)$
\end_inset


\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $(\Leftarrow)$
\end_inset

 Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{+},\ \forall\ z\in\mathbb{R}^{L-1}:\alpha f(z)=f(\alpha z)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Take 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in Y,\ \alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{+}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\emph on
Want to show
\emph default
: 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha y\in Y$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $y\in Y\implies y=(-z,q)\in\mathbb{R}^{L}:z\in\mathbb{R}^{L-1}\ \wedge\ q-f(z)\leq0$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\implies q\leq f(z)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\implies\alpha q\leq\alpha f(z)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\implies\alpha q\leq f(\alpha z)\ \mbox{since}\ \alpha f(z)=f(\alpha z)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Now, 
\begin_inset Formula $(-\alpha z,\alpha q)\in Y\implies\alpha(-z,q)\in Y$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha y\in Y$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ y\in Y,\ \forall\ \alpha\in\mathbb{R}_{+}:\alpha y\in Y$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Q.E.D.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Part*

\bar under
Section 4.4, Problem 3
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider a production set 
\begin_inset Formula $Y\subseteq\mathbb{R}^{L}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi^{Y}$
\end_inset

 be the profit function associated with set 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

, i.e., for each 
\begin_inset Formula $p\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}$
\end_inset

,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\pi^{Y}(p)\equiv{\displaystyle \max_{y\in Y}p\cdot y}
\]

\end_inset


\series bold
(a) Define free disposal and no free lunch.
 Draw a production set that satisfies these two properties.
 Illustrate the profit maximization for some price vector 
\begin_inset Formula $p$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
No-free-lunch
\series default
: 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ y\in Y:\left[y\geq0\implies y=0\right]$
\end_inset

.
 Alternatively, 
\begin_inset Formula $Y\cap\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\subseteq\{0\}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Free-disposal
\series default
: 
\begin_inset Formula $y\in Y\implies\left[\forall\ x\leq y:x\in Y\right]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
(b) Is 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi^{Y}$
\end_inset

 concave, convex, or neither of them?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Claim: 
\series default

\begin_inset Formula $\pi^{Y}(p)$
\end_inset

 is convex
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Take 
\begin_inset Formula $\{\hat{p},\tilde{p}\}\in\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L}\ \mbox{and}\ \alpha\in[0,1]$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}\equiv\arg{\displaystyle \max_{y\in Y}\hat{p}\cdot}y$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\tilde{y}\equiv\arg{\displaystyle \max_{y\in Y}\tilde{p}\cdot y}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha\hat{p}+(1-\alpha)\tilde{p}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $\pi^{Y}(\alpha\hat{p}+(1-\alpha)\tilde{p})=(\alpha\hat{p}+(1-\alpha)\tilde{p})\cdot\bar{y}$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{y}\equiv\arg{\displaystyle \max_{y\in Y}}(\alpha\hat{p}+(1-\alpha)\tilde{p})\cdot y$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $=\alpha\hat{p}\cdot\bar{y}+(1-\alpha)\tilde{p}\cdot\bar{y}\leq\alpha\pi^{Y}(\hat{p})+(1-\alpha)\pi^{Y}(\tilde{p})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Thus, 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall\ \{p,p^{\prime}\}\subseteq\mathbb{R}_{+}^{L},\ \forall\ \alpha\in[0,1]:\pi^{Y}(\alpha p+(1-\alpha)p^{\prime})\leq\alpha\pi^{Y}(p)+(1-\alpha)\pi^{Y}(p^{\prime})$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Therefore, 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi^{Y}(p)$
\end_inset

 is a convex function.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
(c) Suppose now that a firm with production set 
\begin_inset Formula $Y$
\end_inset

 may face an uncertain market.
 The firm is an expected profit maximizer and is risk neutral.
 There are two markets in which the firm may participate
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Market 1: price is uncertain with some probability distribution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize

\series bold
Market 2: price is certian; it is exactly the expected price in Market 1.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Prove that the firm always finds participating in Market 1 as good as participat
ing in Market 2.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset VSpace medskip
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Market 1: 
\begin_inset Formula ${\displaystyle \int}\pi^{Y}(p)dF(p)$
\end_inset

 for some distribution of random price
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Market 2: 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi^{Y}\left({\displaystyle \int}pdF(p)\right)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi^{Y}(p)$
\end_inset

 is a convex function, then by Jensen's inequality, 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi^{Y}\left({\displaystyle \int}pdF(p)\right)\leq{\displaystyle \int}\pi^{Y}(p)dF(p)$
\end_inset


\end_layout

